lement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nCo-design template with compliance, pilot on two active projects this quarter, then publish preprint. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Not trivial low cost? Might be easy? Co-design template is some changes. Could be 3. Maybe 4? Pilot on two projects this quarter is feasible. Probably 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves an implied problem related to stated problem\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nStated problem: lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal converts documentation into reproducibility standard, pilot template. That directly addresses meeting logging mandate. So applicability 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (helps but partial)\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nWill a reproducibility framework solve logging mandate? It provides documentation and logs. It should help meet Article 12. Might be partial because logging capabilities require automatic recording of events, not just documentation. The proposal focuses on documentation of algorithms data provenance training pipelines, which is Recital 71 but Article 12 is about logging capabilities. Might contribute but not fully solve automatic logging? It does co-design research-log template. Could be partial solution. Probably effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solutio