ies paradigm anchored to this problem.\nLevels:\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nThis proposal introduces unified middleware but still treats compliance as logging practice. It converts external cost into infrastructure investment. Might be slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's 2. Could argue paradigm preserving? Standard compliance. Hmm. The description \"converts an external compliance cost into a one-time infrastructure investment that serves every current and future high-risk system\" suggests changing relationship? Might be 3 = changes that relationship (logging woven into science?). Probably 2.\n\noriginality: degree rare but ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nEmbedded logging layer across pipelines is common. So 1 = common, mundane, boring. Evidence: unified logging middleware for compliance is a standard industry practice.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence (one sentence) first and then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says return rating... Provide lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2767\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\netc.\n\nEnsure order: acceptability, implementability, applicability, effectiveness, paradigm_relatedness, originality\n\nProvide one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\nAcceptability evidence: The proposal implements mandatory Article 12 logging fields using standard engineering practice with no legal or normative violations.\n\nImplementability evidence: Adding a standardized middleware module to the existing serving stack within two sprints is feasible but requires coordinated platform engineering and latency testing.\n\nApplicability evidence: It directly captures start/end timestamps, reference-database queries, matched inputs and verifier identities as required by Article 12(3) for high-risk systems.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: If implemented, it would provide automatic event recording over the system lifetime and satisfy traceability and post-market monitoring requirements.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It introduces a reusable logging tool and process but still treats compliance as conventional logging practice rather than reshaping R&D or compliance relationship.\n\nOriginality evidence: A unified logging middleware for regulated AI pipelines is a common, mundane compliance engineering approach.\n\nScores: acceptability 4, implementability 3 (maybe 4?). Let's decide 3. Might be 4 easy? \"next two sprints\" suggests non-radical changes. Could be 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Hmm. I think it's reasonably feasible but not trivially easy. I'll keep 3.\n\nparadigm_relatedness 2\noriginality 1\n\nReturn.\n\n",
