ual engineering. I think contribute partial.\n\nLet's pick 3.\n\nEvidence sentence: Embedding mandatory log-schema and documentation checks at stage gates forces logging requirements to be addressed before deployment, reducing compliance gaps.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related. Do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem. Yes directly addresses how to meet logging mandate. So 4.\n\nEvidence: The gate directly targets record-keeping adequacy required by Article 12 and Recital 71 for high-risk systems.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance...\n\nEmbedding compliance gates is process change but still treats compliance as logging practice. Introduces new elements (reviewer slot) but compliance remains separate checkpoint. That's slightly paradigm stretching? Could be 2. It doesn't weave logging into science, just adds gate. So 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nEvidence: The proposal adds new review checkpoints and reviewer role but keeps compliance as a separate administrative gate rather than integrating logging into research design.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane\n\nStage-gate compliance gates is common practice. Probably common/mundane. So 1.\n