n: degree to which idea will solve problem. It is applied research, not implementation. So maybe effectiveness 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related? Do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem. Proposal addresses Article 12(3) logging mandates for biometric systems. That is directly related. So applicability 4 = solves identified problem directly related.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nEasy to implement at low cost or non-radical changes =4\nSome changes reasonably feasible =3\nSignificant change expensive/difficult but not impossible=2\nTotally infeasible=1\n\nHomomorphic encryption / secure multi-party computation layers over log stores for biometric audit logging. That's significant change, expensive or difficult. Probably 2? Could be 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible. Yes.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies violate no norms =4\nSomewhat uncommon...=3\nOffends sensibilities somewhat=2\nRadically violates=1\n\nPrivacy-preserving logging is socially acceptable, maybe somewhat uncommon but does not offend. So acceptability 3? Could be 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Privacy-preserving is becoming common? Maybe somewhat uncommon. I'd say 3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nProposal converts compliance obligation into open scientific problem in applied cryptography and data governance, with broader impact. That sounds like paradigm breaking? It changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. Yes it converts compliance to research. So 4.\n\nOriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious/imaginative/surprising\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nPrivacy-preserving architectures for biometric audit logging using homomorphic / SMPC. Is that rare? Probably unusual interesting. Might be 3. Could be 4? Not expressed before? Possibly similar ideas exist. I'd say unusual, interesting; shows some imagination =3.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nOrder listed: effectiveness, applicability, implementability, acceptability, paradigm_relatedness, originality\n\nProvide evidence sentence first then score.\n\nWe must ensure one sentence of evidence for each rating.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid P1971\neffectiveness_evidence: ... Then effectiveness 3\netc.\n\nMake sure one sentence each. No extra sentences.\n\nPossibly include JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Probably plain lines.\n\nLet's output as key-value pairs.\n\nEnsure evidence is one sentence.\n\nLet's craft:\n\neffectiveness_evidence: The project aims to meet Article 12(3) logging mandates via privacy-preserving cryptography, which would address compliance if successfully developed, but as an applied research prototype it offers only a partial, future solution rather than immediate implementation.\neffectiveness: 3\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal directly targets the biometric-specific log requirements in Article 12(3) for match events, reference databases and verifier identities tha