mes logging exists. So partial. Score 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nInternal audit program is standard compliance practice. It's paradigm preserving? It introduces audit cell but that's standard. Probably 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged. Or slightly stretching? Introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice => 2. Hmm.\n\nAudit function is fairly standard. Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. The proposal is internal audit. That's common. I'd say 1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged. Actually it does change by adding two-person cell. But still standard.\n\nMaybe 2? Let's choose 1? Let's think: anchored levels. Standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged. Adding quarterly audit is standard practice for regulated labs. So paradigm preserving =1.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is rare but ingenious...\nCommon, mundane, boring =1. Quarterly internal traceability audit is common. So 1.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy low cost non-radical\n3 = some changes or reasonably feasible\n2 = significant change expensive difficult\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nStaff two-per