tation, validate against two partners. That's some changes, reasonably feasible but requires engineering effort. Not easy low cost? Might be 3. Significant change? Build reference middleware is non-trivial. Probably 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible measures.\n\nEvidence: Building and validating a reusable middleware layer requires dedicated engineering and partner coordination beyond routine logging.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree preserves/modifies paradigm anchored to problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners/offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal creates open-source middleware that shifts compliance from bespoke audit plumbing to shared infrastructure, changing relationship between R&D and compliance. Might be paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements tools, but still treats compliance as logging practice. It gives broader community baseline so intellectual energy flows toward model science rather than bespoke plumbing. That changes relationship: logging woven into science via reusable tool and new partners/offerings. Could be 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship. Could also be 2 slightly? It introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. Hmm.\n\nThe description: designed so researchers can plug it in without sacrificing scientific integrity, release open source, validate against partners. That seems like changing relationship: compliance becomes shared infrastructure / offering. I'd say paradigm stretching =3.\n\nEvidence: