re intercepts queries records version hashes etc binds to verifier identities via cryptographic attestation. That seems to directly meet Article 12(1)-(3). Should solve main problem. So 4.\n\nImplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented. Build open-source logging middleware layer that intercepts database queries, records version hashes and match payloads, bind to verifier identities via cryptographic attestation. That's significant change, or expensive/difficult but not totally impossible. Probably some changes reasonably feasible? Might be significant change, expensive/difficult. For an AI R&D lab, building immutable lineage with crypto attestation is non-trivial. Likely 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible. Could be 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Hmm.\n\nAcceptability: degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms? Open-source compliance component is acceptable. 4.\n\nWe need one sentence evidence for each then score.\n\nLet's produce:\n\noriginality_evidence: The proposal combines immutable lineage tracking with cryptographic attestation for biometric matching, an unusual integration beyond standard logging.\noriginality: 3\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It reframes mandatory logging as a publishable reusable architecture, weaving compliance into R&D output and offerings.\nparadigm_relatedness: 3\n\napplicability_evidence: It directly targets Article 12(1) automatic logging and Article 12(3)(b)-(d) requirements for biometric systems.\napplicability: 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: If built, the middleware would automatically record use periods, reference database versions, matching inputs and verifier identities as required.\neffectiveness: 4\n\nimplementability_evidence: Building a production-grade interception layer with cryptographic attestation and open-source release requires substantial engineering effort and integration c