w elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 = paradigm stretching changes that relationship (logging woven into science...). 2 = slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements tools formats processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 = paradigm preserving standard compliance and logging practice; lab way unchanged.\n\nProposal is refactor experiment tracking to include mandatory logging interface. Introduces new element middleware but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's slightly paradigm stretching? Might be 2. It does introduce tool/process but still treats compliance as logging. Could be 1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged. Hmm refactoring infrastructure is a change. I think 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Yes.\n\nEffectiveness: degree will solve problem. Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability =4? It captures required logs. Should solve logging mandate. Might be 4. It addresses Article 12(3) requirements. Yes reasonable and will solve. So 4.\n\nApplicability: degree clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related =4. Do X to get Y and Y is part of stated problem. Yes.\n\nProvide output with id and each subscale evidence sentence then score.\n\nFormat: probably JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\". Might be plain lines.\n\nWe'll produce structured output.\n\nLet's craft sentences:\n\nimplementability_evidence: Refactoring existing experiment-tracking infrastructure to add a mandatory logging interface is a reasonably feasible engineering effort that reuses current pipelines rather than building per-project loggers. Score 3.\n\nacceptability_evidence: A shared logging middleware for compliance is a common, legally required practice that violates no norms or sensibilities. Score 4.\n\noriginality_evidence: Centralized logging middleware for regulatory event capture is a common, mundane compliance engineering approach widely described in industry. Score 1.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal adds a logging tool and process to the existing R&D workflow but keeps compliance as a separate logging practice rather than changing how research is conceived. Score 2.\n\neffectiveness_evidence: Automatic emission of start/end timestamps, reference databases and input matches via the middleware would meet Article 12(1)-(3) traceability requirements for all high-risk systems. Score 4.\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal directly addresses how the lab must meet the automatic logging mandate and post-market monitoring obligations stated in the problem. Score 4.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
