        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence evidence.\n\nWe need rate proposal P1682: Stand Up a Log-Retention and Access Governance Program...\n\nDimensions order: relevance (applicability, effectiveness); workability (acceptability, implementability); novelty (paradigm_relatedness, originality)\n\nSubscales in order: applicability, effectiveness, acceptability, implementability, paradigm_relatedness, originality\n\nProvide evidence sentence then score.\n\nWe need anchored levels exactly.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\nProblem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources...\nProposal is log-retention and access governance program defining retention periods, encryption, RBAC, audit-trail for every log stream... prevents under-retention loses compliance evidence and over-retention creates exposure.\n\nDoes it apply? It solves identified problem directly related to stated problem? Stated problem is how to meet logging mandate. Record-keeping article requires automatic recording of events logs over lifetime. Recital 71 mentions keeping records, technical documentation up to date, allow automatic recording. Proposal addresses retention and access governance for logs. That's directly related: do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. The problem is meet logging mandate. Retention governance is part of compliance. Might be 4.\n\nApplicability 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to the stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Yes.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\nProposal provides governance for retention/access but Article 12 requires automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, enabling recording of events relevant... logging capabilities shall enable recording... For systems in Annex III point 1(a) minimum requirements.\n\nGovernance program doesn't actually implement logging capability; it governs retention. It helps compliance evidence, reduces legal risk, but does it solve logging mandate? The mandate is technically allow automatic recording. Governance is secondary. Might be partial solution. It addresses retention and access, not the creation of logs themselves. Could contribute to traceability and post-market monitoring.\n\nIs it reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? Probably not fully solve; logging capability itself needed. So maybe 3 = contributes to solution but partial.\n\nEvidence: governance ensures logs retained appropriately but does not create automatic recording capability required by Article 12(1).\n\nSo effectiveness = 3?\n\nCould argue 4? The problem is \"how to meet the logging mandate and/or adapt R&D\". Retention governance is part of meeting mandate. Hmm.\n\nMight be 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\nGovernance program with encryption, RBAC, audit trail is common, acceptable. Score 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\nCross-functional working group, embed rules into logging middleware as enforceable config parameters. That's some changes, reasonably feasible but requires cross-functional effort, middleware changes. Might be 3 not 4. Easy? Probably not low cost trivial. It's some changes.\n\nImplementability 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal is standard governance program for logs. It's standard compliance practice. Probably paradigm preserving = 1. Could be slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces policy framework, tiered retention schedules embedded as config. Still treats compliance as logging practice. Might be 1.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting...\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nLog-retention and access governance is common, mundane. Score 1.\n\nReturn rating with id echo.\n\nFormat: maybe JSON? Instruction says for each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\". Probably output list.\n\nI'll output:\n\nid: P1682\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences concise.\n\nApplicability evidence: The proposal creates retention and access rules for log streams generated by high-risk systems, directly addressing how the lab meets Article 12 record-keeping obligations. Score 4\n\nEffectiveness evidence: Governance ensures logs are kept and protected for compliance evidence but does not itself create the automatic event recording capability mandated by Article 12(1)-(3). Score 3\n\nAcceptability evidence: Retention schedules, encryption, RBAC and audit trails are standard, legally aligned data-protection practices that violate no norms. Score 4\n\nImplementability evidence: Establishing a cross-functional working group and embedding enforceable retention parameters into logging middleware requires coordinated changes across policy, IT security and engineering. Score 3\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The program treats compliance as conventional logging administration and leaves the lab’s R&D processes and their relationship to compliance unchanged. Score 1\n\nOriginality evidence: A formal log-retention and access governance program with tiered schedules is a common, mundane compliance measure in regulated labs. Score 1\n\nReturn.\n\n",
