xperienced evaluator expects AI R&D labs to give responses. Scaffolding templates is maybe common practice. Could be common mundane boring =1. Hmm.\n\nThe proposal is pre-built project templates with schemas pre-wired. That's fairly standard DevOps scaffolding. Might be common, mundane. But specifically for Annex III domains? Could be unusual interesting. I'd say originality 2 = interesting. Not rare ingenious. Let's pick 2? Or 3?\n\nMaybe 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. I think it's somewhat interesting but not highly imaginative. I'll go with 2? The instruction: \"not expressed before (rare, unusual) AND ingenious...4\". Unusual interesting shows some imagination 3. I think scaffolding is common. Might be 2.\n\nLet's pick originality = 2 (interesting).\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem.\nProblem: decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Proposal cuts time-to-first-prototype by eliminating week-long schema-design phase. Will it solve logging mandate? It pre-wires paragraph 3 schemas, log emitters. Reasonable and will contribute to solution? It helps implement logging but does not guarantee compliance throughout lifetime, just prototype start. Might be partial solution. Effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Could be 4 = will solve stated problem without regard for workability. Does it solve logging mandate? Provides scaffolding for Annex III domains specifically. May not cover all high-risk systems, just Annex III point1(a). Might be partial. I'd say 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree clearly applies to stated problem.\nSolves identified problem directly related: do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. Yes scaffolds help meet logging mandate. Score 4.\n\nNow output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" one sentence then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON? It says return rating... give ... Probably plain lines. Safer