m.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 = unreasonable...\nProblem is must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nBenchmarking overhead does not actually implement logging, just measures impact. It contributes to decision but doesn't solve compliance. So effectiveness is partial. Might be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Does it solve? It gives numbers on viability before commit architecture decisions. That's helpful but not solving logging mandate. I'd say 3.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\nThe proposal addresses how to meet logging mandate by assessing viability within latency budgets. That's directly related. Might be 4? It solves an identified problem that is directly related: understanding cost of compliance. The stated problem: decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Benchmarking informs adaptation. Probably applicability 3 or 4. I'd say 4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem). Hard numbers on viability before commit architecture decisions. That's directly about meeting mandate. Maybe 3? Let's choose 4.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane\n\nBenchmarking logging overhead is common engineering due diligence. That's mundane. So originality 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to